# static_range_sums:
# CSES 1646, Static Range Sum Queries (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1646), in our
# own words: a list of numbers that does not change, and queries, each a pair of
# places a <= b counted from 1; for each query, the sum of the numbers from place a
# to place b, both included. The input is the list, a Scott list
# (corpora/library/prelude/list) of binary naturals
# (corpora/library/binary/natural), and the queries, a Scott list of pairs `pair a b`
# (corpora/library/prelude/pair) of binary naturals, 1 <= a <= b <= the length of
# the list; the answer is the sum over the queries of each range sum, a binary natural
# (CSES asks the list of the sums; the sum keeps the work and makes the answer one number,
# where the list of 2*10^5 sums at the top rung made a point's answer and its trap
# megabytes). Part of the CSES port's third batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: for each query, the list is walked from
# its first number with the place counted up from 1, adding the numbers whose place is not
# below a, and stopping once the place passes b. The reading takes about b steps a
# query, each step a binary addition; the replacement the family asks for is the
# table of prefix sums, built once in about the length of the list steps, after which
# a query is one subtraction, on fixed-width integers.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application
# only: its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal. It β-reduces
# to V, the canonical binary numeral of the sum of the range sums (each walk consumes
# the finite list of numbers, the sum consumes the finite list of queries); the
# branches that a Scott boolean discards are never read. The program calls no
# subtraction. So ⊢_β P = V, and P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable
# equations for the contextual preorder of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero _ binary_odd _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ merge_binary_increment merge_binary_plus _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "order")
λ binary_less_than _ _
. (λtuple. tuple (binary_odd binary_zero))
λ one
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ numbers first_place last_place
    . fixpoint
      ( λ self place items total
        . items total
          λ number rest
          . binary_less_than last_place place total
            ( self (merge_binary_increment place) rest
              (binary_less_than place first_place total (merge_binary_plus total number))
            )
      ) one numbers binary_zero
  )
λ range_sum
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ numbers queries
    . fixpoint
      ( λ self total rest
        . rest total λ query others. self (merge_binary_plus total (query λfirst_place last_place. range_sum numbers first_place last_place)) others
      ) binary_zero queries
  )
λ static_range_sums
. export static_range_sums
